Let $\Psi$ be a function of $x, y, z$, which within the field is finite
 and continuous and satisfies Laplace's Equation

$$\begin{equation*}
\nabla^{2} \Psi=0 \tag{32}
\end{equation*}$$

and has the constant, but not given, values $\Psi_{1}, \Psi_{2}$, \&c. at the
 surfaces $8_{1}, 8_{2}$, \&c. respectively, and is zero at the external
 surface 8.


The charge of any of the conducting surfaces, as $s_{1}$, is given
 by the surface integral

$$\begin{equation*}
e_{1}=-\frac{1}{4 \pi} \iint \frac{d \Psi}{d v_{1}} d s_{1} \tag{33}
\end{equation*}$$

the normal $\nu_{1}$ being drawn from the surface $\varepsilon_{1}$ into the electric
